,"status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h233","cnm_h230","cnm_h231"],"id":"count-earlier-prefixes","label":"Count the words before USAMO","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-earlier-prefixes":["cnm_h235","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h235","cnm_h234","cnm_h233","cnm_h230","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 600","reason":"There are C(5,3) choices of rows, C(5,3) choices of columns, and 3! matchings. Their product is 10 × 10 × 6 = 600.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h236"],"id":"ordered-blocks-overcount","label":"Count ordered selections and remove repetition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ordered-blocks-overcount":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:double-counting"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:double-counting","cnm_h236"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 112","reason":"For digits x,x+d,x+2d, the possible positive differences 1,2,3,4 give 8+6+4+2=20 sets. Four contain zero. Thus the count is 16·6+4·4=112.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233"],"id":"equally-spaced-digit-sets","label":"Count equally spaced digit sets, then arrange them","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equally-spaced-digit-sets":["cnm_h203","cnm_h235","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h203","cnm_h235","cnm_h232","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 48","reason":"Swapping the first and last pairs bijects heavy-tailed and light-tailed permutations. Equal sums occur only when the middle entry is 1, 3, or 5. Each has one equal-sum partition of the remaining numbers and 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 orders, giving 24 ties. Thus the count is (5! - 24)/2 = 48.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h233","cnm_h231","cnm_p156"],"id":"solution:heavy-light-bijection:58b7569ff084","label":"Pair heavy and light permutations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"heavy-light-bijection":["math:bijective-counting","cnm_h234","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["math:bijective-counting","cnm_h234","cnm_h232","cnm_h233","cnm_h231","cnm_p156"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h233","cnm_h231"],"id":"solution:condition-on-middle-entry:58b7569ff084","label":"Count pair partitions for each middle entry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"condition-on-middle-entry":["cnm_h236","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h236","cnm_h232","cnm_h233","cnm_h231"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 900","reason":"The six matchings split into the three even and three odd permutations. Each player pairing belongs to exactly one matching in each group, and every even-odd matching pair shares one pairing. Consequently, matching multiplicities are constant within each group, say a and b, with a+b=2. The possibilities give 6!/(2!)^3, 6!, and 6!/(2!)^3 schedules, totaling 90+720+90=900.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:factorial-notation"],"id":"one-player-first","label":"Schedule one player first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"one-player-first":["math:multiset-permutations","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["math:multiset-permutations","cnm_h232","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:factorial-notation"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:factorial-notation"],"id":"round-type-multiplicities","label":"Classify the six possible types of round","methodDirectNodeIds":{"round-type-multiplicities":["math:multiset-permutations","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["math:multiset-permutations","cnm_h232","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:factorial-notation"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 9; the exact average is 9.1","reason":"There are 7×13 = 91 boy-girl pairs. Each is adjacent in 2×19! of the 20! orders, so the average is 91×2×19!/20! = 9.1, closest to 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:factorial-notation"],"id":"count-person-pairs","label":"Count orders containing a chosen adjacent pair","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-person-pairs":["cnm_211","math:double-counting","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_211","math:double-counting","cnm_h234","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","math:factorial-notation"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 40","reason":"Because 2310=2·3·5·7·11 is squarefree, there are 3^5=243 ordered triples. Equal entries can only be two 1s, giving three excluded triples. Each remaining set has six orderings, so the count is (243−3)/6=40.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_h002"],"id":"1995_AMC_12_Q29_m1","label":"Count ordered factorizations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1995_AMC_12_Q29_m1":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-factorization","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_h002"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 5/256","reason":"Partition vertices into three opposite pairs. No ant can move within its own pair. If k ants from the first pair go to the second pair, destination capacities force all six inter-pair flow counts. For each k=0,1,2 there are C(2,k)^3 choices of ants and (2!)^3 assignments within destination pairs. Thus there are 8(1+8+1)=80 valid moves among 4^6=4096 equally likely moves, giving 5/256.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_h140"],"id":"opposite-pair-counting","label":"Group the vertices into opposite pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"opposite-pair-counting":["cnm_h137","cnm_h232","math:graph-models"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h137","cnm_h232","math:graph-models","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_h140"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (132)","reason":"If exactly three songs have pair types, choose the exceptional song in 4 ways, its liking subset in 4 ways, and arrange the three required pair types in 3! ways, giving 96. If all four have pair types, choose the repeated type in 3 ways and its two positions in 6 ways, then order the other types in 2 ways, giving 36. Total: 132.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_h237"],"id":"classify-number-of-pair-songs","label":"Method 1: Count three or four songs liked by pairs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"classify-number-of-pair-songs":["cnm_h232","cnm_h239","cnm_h234"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h232","cnm_h239","cnm_h234","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_h237"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2220","reason":"There are 5^5=3125 unrestricted assignments. At most one room can contain at least three guests. The invalid count is 5[C(5,3)·4²+C(5,4)·4+1]=905, leaving 3125−905=2220.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h231","cnm_h236","cnm_h233"],"id":"classify-two-person-rooms","label":"Classify by the number of shared rooms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"classify-two-person-rooms":["cnm_h232","cnm_h239","cnm_h235"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h232","cnm_h239","cnm_h235","cnm_h231","cnm_h236","cnm_h233"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 8178","reason":"For a fixed first-pass endpoint k, where 1≤k≤12, there are C(13,k) interleavings of the increasing groups 1,...,k and k+1,...,13. Exactly one interleaving is completely increasing and must be excluded. Every valid arrangement has a unique endpoint k, so the count is Σ[C(13,k)-1]=2¹³-2-12=8178.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h230","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_h236"],"id":"method_1","label":"Method 1: Choose positions for the two passes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method_1":["cnm_h232","math:bijective-counting","cnm_h239","cnm_h242"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h232","math:bijective-counting","cnm_h239","cnm_h242","cnm_h230","cnm_h231","cnm_h233","cnm_h236"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h230","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"],"id":"method_2","label":"Method 2: Count by inserting the largest entry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method_2":["cnm_h232","math:bijective-counting","math:recursive-counting-probability"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h232","math:bijective-counting","math:recursive-counting-probability","cnm_h230","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 6 ordered triples","reason":"Set X=x, Y=2y, Z=4z. Then X+Y+Z=12, XY+YZ+ZX=44, and XYZ=48. These are the roots of t³-12t²+44t-48=(t-2)(t-4)(t-6). The three distinct roots have 3!=6 assignments, each giving a unique original triple.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"],"id":"scaled-symmetric-root-polynomial","label":"Rescale the variables and recover them as roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"scaled-symmetric-root-polynomial":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_146","cnm_029","cnm_032","cnm_h231","cnm_h233"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 19","reason":"There are 4·4! = 96 allowed permutations. With second term 2, the first term has 3 choices and the remaining terms have 3! arrangements, giving 18 favorable permutations. The probability is 3/16, so a + b = 19.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_309","cnm_h233","cnm_h234","cnm_p168"],"id":"count-allowed-and-favorable","label":"Count the allowed and favorable permutations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"count-allowed-and-favorable":["cnm_h137","cnm_h235","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h137","cnm_h235","cnm_h231","cnm_309","cnm_h233","cnm_h234","cnm_p168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 6","reason":"Every face must sum to 18. Subtracting face equations shows that the four body-diagonal pairs have one common signed difference when oriented from one checkerboard tetrahedron to the other. Pairing 1 through 8 at a fixed positive gap permits only gaps 1, 2, and 4. For each gap, choosing the tetrahedron for the smaller labels and permuting those four labels gives 2·4! = 48 valid labeled cubes. Dividing by the 24 rotations gives 2 classes per gap, hence 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h233"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p160","cnm_h233","cnm_040"],"id":"opposite-pairs-and-rotations","label":"Opposite pairs and rotations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"opposite-pairs-and-rotations":["math:symmetry-counting","math:finite-constraint-deduction","cnm_h234","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["math:symmetry-counting","math:finite-constraint-deduction","cnm_h234","cnm_h232","cnm_p160","cnm_h233","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b6e1f5b7f8e38ef277334054"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 170","reason":"Degrees 0 and 5 are excluded. There are 5*3*1=15 degree-1 graphs. A degree-2 graph is either one 6-cycle, giving 5!/2=60 choices, or two disjoint triangles, giving C(6,3)/2=10 choices. Complementation gives 15 degree-4 and 70 degree-3 graphs. The total is 2*(15+70)=170.","status":"matches"},